<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	This file is a record, not part of the .ipa.

	The .ipa built by build-ipa.sh is unsigned on purpose: it is the input to
	TrollStore, which installs without checking a signature at all, and to
	Sideloadly / SideStore, which re-sign with the installer's own Apple ID and
	replace this dictionary wholesale while doing so.

	Signing it here instead would break the install. A restricted entitlement
	without a provisioning profile is refused, which was measured on the
	simulator: a bundle ad-hoc signed with `application-identifier` is denied by
	SBMainWorkspace, while the same bundle unsigned launches.

	What is listed is what a build signed by Xcode is granted for free, and what
	`AppStores` probes for before it decides between the Keychain and the file
	fallback:

	  * `application-identifier` — the check the Keychain makes on every call.
	    Its value is the signing team's prefix plus the bundle ID, which is why
	    no literal is written here.
	  * `keychain-access-groups` — the group the node's Keychain item lives in.
	-->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>app.camellia.mobile.diagnostics</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
